---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AustraliaCalculator from '../../components/AustraliaCalculator.tsx';
---

<Layout title="Australia SkillSelect Points Calculator 2026 | ImmigrationPoints.com" description="Free Australia SkillSelect points calculator for skilled migration visas. Calculate your points for subclass 189, 190, and 491 visas.">
  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="bg-gradient-to-br from-primary to-primary-dark text-white py-12">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 text-center">
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-4">Australia SkillSelect Points Calculator</h1>
      <p class="text-lg opacity-90 max-w-2xl mx-auto">
        Calculate your points for Australia's General Skilled Migration program. Check if you meet the 65-point pass mark for skilled visas. Updated for 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Calculator -->
  <section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <AustraliaCalculator client:load />
  </section>

  <!-- Content -->
  <section class="bg-neutral-100 py-16">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 prose prose-lg">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mb-6">Complete Guide to Australia's SkillSelect Points System</h2>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Australia's General Skilled Migration (GSM) program is one of the most popular pathways for skilled workers seeking to live and work in Australia. The program uses a points-based system administered through SkillSelect, an online platform where prospective migrants submit their Expressions of Interest (EOI) and are ranked based on their points score. Understanding how the points system works is crucial for anyone considering skilled migration to Australia.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-8 mb-4">The SkillSelect Process</h3>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        The SkillSelect process begins with identifying your skilled occupation on the relevant Skilled Occupation List (SOL) and obtaining a positive skills assessment from the relevant assessing authority for your occupation. Once you have a valid skills assessment and meet the minimum requirements, you can submit an Expression of Interest through SkillSelect. Your EOI is then scored using the points test, and you are placed in the selection pool.
      </p>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        The Department of Home Affairs conducts regular invitation rounds, typically monthly, inviting the highest-scoring candidates to apply for a visa. The minimum pass mark is 65 points, but in practice, many occupations require significantly higher scores due to high demand and limited invitation places. Some competitive occupations such as accounting, IT, and engineering may see minimum effective scores well above 80 points.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-8 mb-4">Understanding the Points Categories</h3>

      <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-6 mb-3">Age</h4>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Age is one of the most significant factors in the points test. The maximum of 30 points is awarded to applicants aged 25 to 32 at the time of invitation. Applicants aged 18 to 24 and 33 to 39 receive 25 points, while those aged 40 to 44 receive 15 points. Applicants aged 45 to 49 receive no age points, and generally those aged 45 and over are not eligible for most skilled visa subclasses (with some exceptions for certain visa pathways). This age-based scoring reflects Australia's emphasis on attracting younger workers who will contribute to the economy and tax base for many years.
      </p>

      <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-6 mb-3">English Language Ability</h4>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        English language ability is assessed through standardized testing and contributes significantly to your points score. Superior English, defined as an IELTS score of 8 or above in all four components (or equivalent in PTE Academic, TOEFL iBT, or Cambridge C1 Advanced), earns 20 points. Proficient English, equivalent to IELTS 7 or above in all components, earns 10 points. Competent English, the minimum required for most skilled visas and equivalent to IELTS 6 or above, earns 0 additional points but is still required as a baseline.
      </p>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Investing in English language preparation can be one of the most cost-effective ways to increase your points score. Moving from competent to proficient English adds 10 points, and moving from proficient to superior adds another 10 points. Many applicants find that focused preparation for the IELTS, PTE, or other accepted tests can yield significant improvements in their overall score.
      </p>

      <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-6 mb-3">Skilled Employment</h4>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Work experience in your nominated skilled occupation is divided into overseas and Australian categories, with Australian experience receiving more favorable scoring. For overseas work experience, three to four years earns 5 points, five to seven years earns 10 points, and eight or more years earns 15 points. Experience of less than three years overseas does not earn any points.
      </p>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Australian skilled work experience is valued more highly. Even one to two years of Australian experience earns 5 points, three to four years earns 10 points, five to seven years earns 15 points, and eight or more years earns the maximum of 20 points. This premium reflects the practical reality that Australian employers highly value local experience, and immigrants with Australian work history tend to integrate more quickly into the labor market.
      </p>

      <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-6 mb-3">Educational Qualifications</h4>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Education qualifications are assessed based on the level of your highest completed qualification that is relevant to your nominated occupation. A doctoral degree from an Australian institution or equivalent overseas institution earns 20 points. A bachelor's degree earns 15 points, while a diploma, advanced diploma, or trade qualification earns 10 points. Qualifications must be assessed by the relevant skills assessing authority and, for overseas qualifications, may need to be evaluated for Australian equivalence.
      </p>

      <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-6 mb-3">Bonus Points</h4>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Several bonus factors can add points to your total score. The Australian Study Requirement (ASR) adds 5 points if you have completed one or more degrees, diplomas, or trade qualifications in Australia that took at least two academic years of full-time study. Specialist education qualifications in science, technology, engineering, mathematics (STEM), or information and communications technology (ICT) fields can earn an additional 5 points.
      </p>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Holding an accreditation from the National Accreditation Authority for Translators and Interpreters (NAATI) adds 5 points, recognizing the value of community language skills. Completing a Professional Year program in Australia in accounting, IT, or engineering adds 5 points. These Professional Year programs typically run for 44 weeks and combine formal learning with workplace experience.
      </p>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Partner skills can contribute 5 to 10 points depending on your partner's qualifications. If your partner has competent English and a skills assessment in an occupation on the relevant skilled occupation list, you receive 10 points. If your partner has only competent English (without a skills assessment), you receive 5 points. Single applicants or those with partners who are Australian citizens or permanent residents also receive 10 points in this category.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-8 mb-4">State and Regional Nominations</h3>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        State and territory nominations are important pathways for applicants who may not reach competitive scores for the subclass 189 (independent) visa. A state or territory nomination for the subclass 190 visa adds 5 points to your score, while a regional nomination or sponsorship for the subclass 491 visa adds 15 points. Each state and territory has its own nomination criteria, which may include specific occupation lists, work experience requirements, English language thresholds, and commitments to live and work in that state or territory.
      </p>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        The subclass 491 visa, in particular, has become an increasingly popular pathway. It provides a provisional visa for five years, during which holders must live, work, and study in a designated regional area. After three years on the subclass 491 visa and meeting income requirements, holders can apply for the subclass 191 permanent residence visa. The generous 15-point bonus for regional nomination makes this pathway accessible to a broader range of applicants.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-8 mb-4">Visa Subclasses Explained</h3>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        The three main visa subclasses under the GSM program are the subclass 189 (Skilled Independent), subclass 190 (Skilled Nominated), and subclass 491 (Skilled Work Regional). The subclass 189 does not require nomination or sponsorship and provides permanent residence immediately upon grant. The subclass 190 requires state or territory nomination and also provides permanent residence. The subclass 491 requires regional nomination or sponsorship and provides a five-year provisional visa with a pathway to permanent residence.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-800 mt-8 mb-4">Tips for Maximizing Your Score</h3>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        To maximize your points score, focus on the areas where you can realistically improve. Language testing is often the most accessible lever — investing in test preparation to move from competent to proficient or superior English can add 10 to 20 points. If you have the opportunity to gain Australian work experience, even one year adds 5 points and can also strengthen your overall application.
      </p>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Consider pursuing the Australian Study Requirement if you are already in Australia, as this adds 5 points and can also qualify you for the post-study work visa. Explore state and territory nomination programs, particularly for regional areas, which can add 5 to 15 points and may have less competitive criteria than the independent subclass 189 pathway.
      </p>

      <p class="text-neutral-700 leading-relaxed">
        Remember that your EOI in SkillSelect is valid for two years. During this period, your score may change if your circumstances change — for example, if you gain additional work experience or achieve a higher English test score. It is important to keep your EOI up to date to ensure you receive any invitations you may be eligible for. Use this calculator regularly to model different scenarios and plan your strategy for achieving the highest possible score.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
